# Line endings for the two start scripts, pinned rather than left to whatever
# core.autocrlf each contributor happens to have set.
#
# This is not tidiness.  A shell script checked out with CRLF endings has a
# carriage return stuck on the end of its shebang, so the kernel looks for an
# interpreter called "/bin/sh\r", does not find one, and reports the confusing
# "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".  On a Windows clone with
# autocrlf=true that is exactly what would happen to start-pokepack.sh.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Batch files are the mirror image: cmd.exe is happiest with CRLF, and can
# mis-read labels and goto targets without it.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
